<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lakshadweep</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Laccadive Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lakshadweep is a group of coral islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, forming a union territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Formerly called the Laccadive Islands in English, most of them were long held by the Kolathiri rulers and then the Arakkal family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Cannanore</a>, until the British took control in the 19th century. Most of its people are Muslims speaking Malayalam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lakshadweep.jpg" alt="Lakshadweep" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manvendra Bhangui from Chennai, India · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_beach_side_resort_at_Kadmat_Island,_Lakshadweep.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It covered the southernmost part of the Indian subcontinent: present-day Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Puducherry, <b>Lakshadweep</b> and southern parts of Andhra Pradesh and Karnataka.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 16th century, as the Portuguese disrupted the coast, they became an autonomous power, holding the title Ali Raja, lord of the sea, and ruling the town and the <b>Lakshadweep</b> islands.</blockquote><a href="/state/arakkal-kingdom">Arakkal kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
